    {
      "id": "common-comparison-mask",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/common-comparison-mask.md",
      "sha256": "bbb98200f52b4a692ce097704b8c438179a795a44bec6b856c03cf31931af4ea",
      "reason": "The SSS bias, RMSE and spatial correlation against WOA23 must be computed only over cells both fields cover, using one common mask and not applying WOA23's land-sea mask where WOA23 is not part of the comparison.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Put all three models and WOA23 on the one 1° grid, build one mask with `build_comparison_mask` and apply it with `.where(mask)` to every field before the bias maps, RMSE and pattern correlation; WOA23 is the reference here, so the default `ocean_only=True` is the right choice.",
        "Report the cell counts the mask records alongside the metrics; a mask much smaller than the individual fields means the products disagree about where the ocean is, not that the models are poor."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "historical-ssp245-member-continuity",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "pitfalls/historical-ssp245-member-continuity.md",
      "sha256": "02341c56015c6296db14585b4561fc32ca6b4e52e2264bec50eee6746c1d55f6",
      "reason": "The requested historical-to-ssp245 ratio and period changes must state the ensemble-member caveat, since CESM2 ssp245 continues r10i1p1f1 while the local CESM2 historical is r1i1p1f1.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "CESM2 ssp245 is r10i1p1f1 (parent historical r10i1p1f1) but only historical r1 is local, so the CESM2 delta SSS (ssp245 2071-2100 minus historical 1995-2014) contains forced change plus the internal-variability difference between two members — state this caveat; GFDL-ESM4 and MPI-ESM1-2-LR are r1/r1 and continuous.",
        "Do not fit a trend across the 2014/2015 join for CESM2; the two segments are fitted separately."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices.md",
      "sha256": "e0afb5c187aca1e075f9549153b1e896320bb0539544f66d3293fab4b4b77c92",
      "reason": "The mean-state bias and delta SSS depend on the stated baselines—WOA23's 1971-2000 normal and the historical 1995-2014 versus ssp245 2071-2100 periods—so offsets and baseline definitions must be stated.",
      "status": "not_applicable",
      "not_applicable_reason": "no climatology removal for anomalies, no ENSO index, no season convention or event threshold is involved; SSS metrics use the raw fields against the WOA23 1971-2000 normal."
    },
    {
      "id": "basin-and-region-definitions",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/basin-and-region-definitions.md",
      "sha256": "d0d90c32fab9f6e0a39d4f769a4e902e6412b2ad1b09d9c7d19f6bb3aa426611",
      "reason": "The Atlantic, Indian and Pacific SSS changes depend on the basin partition and marginal-sea treatment, so the WOA23 basin-mask definition and exclusions must be stated.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Basin means use the WOA23 mask (`select_basin`, `load_basin_mask`) with marginal seas folded in; state that it follows WOA labelling, not the OMIP 10-region one, and how the Southern/Arctic Oceans are treated.",
        "Marginal seas in the WOA mask may have no model counterpart (CESM2 drops the Caspian), so the cells behind a basin mean differ between sources."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-weighted-means-and-integrals",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-weighted-means-and-integrals.md",
      "sha256": "89de52676ae54db8f5897bc77a08c78de3f776696db0020935a38b790c302cbe",
      "reason": "Global SSS means, including the 0-10 m level average and annual global series, must use the appropriate cell weights rather than an unweighted average over cells or levels.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Global and basin mean SSS = Σ SSS·areacello / Σ areacello over valid cells only (NaN-aware weights); use `compute_basic_stat` with `op='area_weighted_mean'`, never a plain `mean()`.",
        "`compute_difference_metrics` weights by area by default; `analyze_correlation` needs `weighting='area'` for the spatial pattern correlation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-so-practical-salinity",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-so-practical-salinity.md",
      "sha256": "96f2763290996040d675ca6e8f997ea29fa320b8f6e6793bf2c6fcb739345e19",
      "reason": "CMIP6 `so` is practical salinity and should be compared in psu, while any TEOS-10 calculation would require Absolute Salinity derived from it.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "`so` and WOA23 salinity are both practical salinity: units \"0.001\" and \"1\" denote the same number of order 35 — difference them directly, no ×0.001, and label results psu."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "serial-correlation-and-significance",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/serial-correlation-and-significance.md",
      "sha256": "fe11bd008257a589f44e369faba5fa31ab3cf9ad99280e88c763ac45d2b0e917",
      "reason": "The annual global SSS series are autocorrelated, so the trend confidence intervals, R-squared, and stippled significance maps need effective sample size or otherwise account for serial correlation.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "`fit_1D_trend` and `fit_trend_map` apply an effective sample size along time by default; quote the corrected confidence interval and p, and call a trend significant only if the interval excludes zero (stipple those cells).",
        "No such correction exists for the spatial pattern correlation: read its coefficient, not its p-value."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-native-grid-vs-regridded",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-native-grid-vs-regridded.md",
      "sha256": "8ff046ffbadc5794fcd2f8fe93d3bb947c736dec650aa3a3cd6e2412b7da8e68",
      "reason": "Regridding changes land-sea masks and breaks conservation, so global means and period changes should be computed on native grids and regridding used only for maps and pattern comparisons against WOA23.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Compute every number (global and basin mean SSS, delta SSS, the global-mean trends) on each model's own grid with its own `areacello`; regrid to the WOA 1° grid only for the bias/RMSE maps and pattern correlation, and state the method ('conservative').",
        "The cell-by-cell trend belongs on the field's own grid; the figure-2 map is a regridded copy for display, and a regridded RMSE or pattern correlation depends on the regridding method."
      ]
